#N canvas 40 60 1280 1030 12;
#X declare -path ..;
#X obj 0 0 cnv 1 1280 46 empty empty 08\ \ \ Networks\ as\ mappings\ \ -\ \ two\ knobs\,\ one\ instrument 14 23 0 20 #1d3557 #ffffff 0;
#X obj 1110 1000 declare -path ..;
#X text 800 1000 finds the nn.* objects one folder up ->, f 42;
#X text 20 1000 neuralplay - Moisés Horta Valenzuela / SEMILLA.AI Studio, f 70;
#X text 20 56 A small random network turns 2 gestures (X \, Y) into 4 synthesis parameters. Every point of the XY plane is a sound \, nearby points sound alike (the network is smooth) \, and "randomize" builds a new instrument. This is how neural networks enter instrument design: as MAPPINGS., f 175;
#X obj 10 96 cnv 1 390 650 empty empty THE\ IDEA 8 12 0 13 #eef1f5 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 122 MAPPING - connecting a few controls to many sound parameters - decides how an instrument feels (Hunt and Wanderley \, 2002). One-to-one mappings are easy to learn but flat \, many-to-many mappings are expressive., f 52;
#X text 20 203 A random network is a rich many-to-many mapping that comes for free. Each neuron bends the XY plane once (its curve) \, the next layer bends the bends. 2 inputs -> 5 -> 5 -> 4 outputs here: 14 neurons \, 80 weights., f 52;
#X text 20 284 Rebecca Fiebrink's Wekinator (2009) TRAINS such networks from examples: put the knobs somewhere \, play the sound you want there \, repeat \, train. RAVE (2021) learns a whole LATENT SPACE in which every point is a sound (see lesson 11)., f 52;
#X text 20 380 SEED: randomize is repeatable. "seed 42" always rebuilds the same instrument - write down the seeds you like., f 52;
#X text 20 431 TRY: switch all neurons to sin (folding mappings \, more chaotic) or relu (piecewise \, with sudden edges). Raise the spread: bigger weights = more saturated \, more switch-like mappings., f 52;
#X obj 410 96 cnv 1 860 650 empty empty THE\ NETWORK\ \ (net\ name:\ l08) 8 12 0 13 #e3f0fb #1d3557 0;
#X obj 430 140 hsl 180 15 -1 1 0 1 empty l08-x X 0 -8 0 11 #fcfcfc #000000 #000000 6300 1;
#X floatatom 618 139 7 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 430 180 hsl 180 15 -1 1 0 1 empty l08-y Y 0 -8 0 11 #fcfcfc #000000 #000000 12500 1;
#X floatatom 618 179 7 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 700 136 \$1 40;
#X obj 700 162 line~;
#X msg 780 136 \$1 40;
#X obj 780 162 line~;
#X text 700 190 ^ smooth the knobs, f 20;
#X obj 430 220 tgl 19 0 empty l08-explore explore:\ wander\ around\ the\ XY\ plane\ by\ itself 24 10 0 12 #fcfcfc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#N canvas 40 60 900 400 random-walk 0;
#X text 20 10 EXPLORE: every 60 ms take a small random step from the current position (a random walk) and move the sliders - they then drive the network as if you moved them. float() matters: [expr] treats random() as an integer., f 110;
#X obj 20 60 inlet;
#X obj 20 86 metro 60;
#X obj 20 140 f;
#X obj 80 112 r \$0-x;
#X obj 20 166 expr min(max(\$f1 + float(random(-100 \, 101)) / 1500 \, -1) \, 1), f 34;
#X obj 20 210 s l08-x;
#X obj 320 140 f;
#X obj 380 112 r \$0-y;
#X obj 320 166 expr min(max(\$f1 + float(random(-100 \, 101)) / 1500 \, -1) \, 1), f 34;
#X obj 320 210 s l08-y;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 4 0 3 1;
#X connect 3 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 2 0 7 0;
#X connect 8 0 7 1;
#X connect 7 0 9 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X restore 430 250 pd random-walk;
#X obj 580 250 s \$0-x;
#X obj 660 250 s \$0-y;
#X floatatom 430 296 5 0 0 1 seed - - 0;
#X msg 530 290 \; nn-l08 seed \$1 \; nn-l08 randomize;
#X msg 900 136 \; nn-l08 randomize;
#X msg 1034 136 \; nn-l08 act tanh;
#X msg 900 180 \; nn-l08 act sin;
#X msg 1020 180 \; nn-l08 act relu;
